1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,273
Two teams. Both say they are great at DevOps.

2
00:00:03,823 --> 00:00:05,589
One ships many times a week,

3
00:00:05,589 --> 00:00:07,607
and fixes problems in two hours.

4
00:00:07,607 --> 00:00:09,499
The other ships twice a month,

5
00:00:09,499 --> 00:00:10,445
in a big batch,

6
00:00:10,445 --> 00:00:12,399
and fixes problems in two days.

7
00:00:12,949 --> 00:00:16,085
Who is better? Opinions will not settle it. Numbers will.

8
00:00:16,635 --> 00:00:18,921
Today: the four key DevOps metrics.

9
00:00:18,921 --> 00:00:22,096
Four numbers that show how a team really performs,

10
00:00:22,096 --> 00:00:24,635
and how you can calculate them yourself.

11
00:00:26,185 --> 00:00:27,614
Here is what to expect.

12
00:00:28,164 --> 00:00:30,895
One: why we measure, and what makes a good metric.

13
00:00:31,445 --> 00:00:34,517
Two: the two speed metrics, frequency and lead time.

14
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:39,056
Three: the two stability metrics, failure rate and time to restore.

15
00:00:39,606 --> 00:00:45,324
Four: a live demo. We calculate all four, from a real deploy log, for two different teams.

16
00:00:45,874 --> 00:00:49,180
This is lesson six of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

17
00:00:51,230 --> 00:00:52,275
Start with why.

18
00:00:52,825 --> 00:00:56,456
In the last lesson we met the error budget: reliability,

19
00:00:56,456 --> 00:00:57,817
turned into a number.

20
00:00:57,817 --> 00:01:01,188
The same idea applies to the whole delivery process.

21
00:01:01,738 --> 00:01:02,725
Without numbers,

22
00:01:02,725 --> 00:01:04,266
improvement is guesswork.

23
00:01:04,266 --> 00:01:07,473
Everyone has a feeling about where the slow part is,

24
00:01:07,473 --> 00:01:09,077
and the feelings disagree.

25
00:01:09,627 --> 00:01:13,030
A research group called DORA studied thousands of teams,

26
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:16,008
and found four measures that predict performance.

27
00:01:16,008 --> 00:01:18,561
Teams that score well on them ship faster,

28
00:01:18,561 --> 00:01:20,080
and are also more stable.

29
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:21,981
Notice that last part.

30
00:01:21,981 --> 00:01:24,684
Speed and stability do not fight each other.

31
00:01:24,684 --> 00:01:26,219
The best teams have both.

32
00:01:27,769 --> 00:01:29,646
The first two metrics measure speed.

33
00:01:30,196 --> 00:01:32,259
Metric one: deployment frequency.

34
00:01:32,259 --> 00:01:34,696
How often do you release to production?

35
00:01:34,696 --> 00:01:35,196
Per day,

36
00:01:35,196 --> 00:01:35,758
per week,

37
00:01:35,758 --> 00:01:36,383
per month.

38
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:39,606
Frequent releases mean small releases.

39
00:01:39,606 --> 00:01:41,927
Small changes are easier to test,

40
00:01:41,927 --> 00:01:43,404
easier to understand,

41
00:01:43,404 --> 00:01:44,741
and easier to undo.

42
00:01:45,291 --> 00:01:47,493
Metric two: lead time for changes.

43
00:01:47,493 --> 00:01:50,213
How long from a developer committing code,

44
00:01:50,213 --> 00:01:52,480
to that code running for customers?

45
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:55,398
Short lead time means fast feedback,

46
00:01:55,398 --> 00:01:56,384
and fast fixes.

47
00:01:56,384 --> 00:01:59,673
Long lead time usually hides waiting: for reviews,

48
00:01:59,673 --> 00:02:00,593
for approvals,

49
00:02:00,593 --> 00:02:01,777
for a release day.

50
00:02:03,327 --> 00:02:05,418
The next two metrics measure stability.

51
00:02:05,968 --> 00:02:08,126
Metric three: change failure rate.

52
00:02:08,126 --> 00:02:09,460
Of all your releases,

53
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:12,063
what share cause a problem in production?

54
00:02:12,063 --> 00:02:12,761
A rollback,

55
00:02:12,761 --> 00:02:13,333
a hotfix,

56
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:13,968
an outage.

57
00:02:14,518 --> 00:02:16,480
Metric four: time to restore.

58
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:18,239
When something does break,

59
00:02:18,239 --> 00:02:21,148
how long until customers are working again?

60
00:02:21,148 --> 00:02:22,974
Some teams call it MTTR,

61
00:02:22,974 --> 00:02:24,395
mean time to recover.

62
00:02:24,945 --> 00:02:26,329
Failures will happen.

63
00:02:26,329 --> 00:02:28,042
The question is how often,

64
00:02:28,042 --> 00:02:29,887
and how quickly you recover.

65
00:02:29,887 --> 00:02:31,402
That is why automation,

66
00:02:31,402 --> 00:02:31,798
tests,

67
00:02:31,798 --> 00:02:33,116
and rollback matter.

68
00:02:33,666 --> 00:02:40,450
And here is the connection to the pipeline: every earlier lesson in this domain pushes at least one of these four numbers.

69
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,453
Read the four numbers together, never alone.

70
00:02:45,003 --> 00:02:48,709
High frequency with a high failure rate means you are rushing.

71
00:02:48,709 --> 00:02:54,027
Low failure rate with a very slow lead time means you are being careful in the wrong way.

72
00:02:54,577 --> 00:02:55,438
Two warnings.

73
00:02:55,438 --> 00:02:55,836
First,

74
00:02:55,836 --> 00:02:58,418
these numbers describe a team's system,

75
00:02:58,418 --> 00:02:59,279
not a person.

76
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:02,193
Never use them to rank or blame individuals.

77
00:03:02,743 --> 00:03:03,165
Second,

78
00:03:03,165 --> 00:03:05,031
when a number becomes a target,

79
00:03:05,031 --> 00:03:05,934
people game it.

80
00:03:05,934 --> 00:03:08,343
Use the metrics to start a conversation,

81
00:03:08,343 --> 00:03:10,871
and to see whether a change really helped.

82
00:03:11,421 --> 00:03:16,413
Pick one number to improve, change one thing, and measure again. That is the whole method.

83
00:03:16,963 --> 00:03:20,377
In the next lesson, we use these numbers to find the bottleneck.

84
00:03:21,927 --> 00:03:25,852
Go to the demo folder.

85
00:03:27,402 --> 00:03:29,301
Look around.

86
00:03:29,851 --> 00:03:33,584
Two deploy logs, and one script that calculates the four metrics.

87
00:03:34,134 --> 00:03:37,718
The deploy log.

88
00:03:38,268 --> 00:03:40,759
One row per release: when it was committed,

89
00:03:40,759 --> 00:03:41,802
when it went live,

90
00:03:41,802 --> 00:03:42,845
whether it failed,

91
00:03:42,845 --> 00:03:44,583
and when service was restored.

92
00:03:45,133 --> 00:03:50,424
Calculate.

93
00:03:50,974 --> 00:03:53,103
Twelve deployments in four weeks,

94
00:03:53,103 --> 00:03:53,941
three a week.

95
00:03:53,941 --> 00:03:54,586
Lead time,

96
00:03:54,586 --> 00:03:56,199
15.5 hours.

97
00:03:56,199 --> 00:03:57,038
Failure rate,

98
00:03:57,038 --> 00:03:58,199
17 percent,

99
00:03:58,199 --> 00:03:59,102
two of twelve.

100
00:03:59,102 --> 00:04:00,393
And time to restore,

101
00:04:00,393 --> 00:04:01,683
2.2 hours.

102
00:04:02,233 --> 00:04:08,292
The second team.

103
00:04:08,842 --> 00:04:12,959
Only four rows. Big batches, committed days before they go live.

104
00:04:13,509 --> 00:04:16,176
Calculate again.

105
00:04:17,526 --> 00:04:18,978
One deployment a week.

106
00:04:18,978 --> 00:04:19,638
Lead time,

107
00:04:19,638 --> 00:04:22,806
262 hours: more than ten days.

108
00:04:22,806 --> 00:04:24,522
Half of all releases fail,

109
00:04:24,522 --> 00:04:27,360
and it takes almost 40 hours to restore.

110
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:32,646
Same company, same tools. The numbers tell the story, without any opinions.

111
00:04:33,196 --> 00:04:36,844
Write it down.

112
00:04:47,294 --> 00:04:49,876
Check the file.

113
00:04:51,426 --> 00:04:53,687
Your practice task: measure two teams.

114
00:04:54,237 --> 00:04:57,971
One: look at the first deploy log, and guess which team is healthier.

115
00:04:58,521 --> 00:05:02,403
Two: run the script on both logs, and compare all four numbers.

116
00:05:02,953 --> 00:05:05,332
Three: change one row in the first log,

117
00:05:05,332 --> 00:05:06,551
make a release fail,

118
00:05:06,551 --> 00:05:07,588
and run it again.

119
00:05:07,588 --> 00:05:08,991
Watch the numbers move.

120
00:05:09,541 --> 00:05:11,119
Four: write your four lines.

121
00:05:13,169 --> 00:05:16,390
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

122
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:22,615
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

123
00:05:23,165 --> 00:05:26,984
Two: the folder academy/lesson150-demo.

124
00:05:27,534 --> 00:05:32,334
Three: two deploy logs, one script called metrics, and a read me with the answers.

125
00:05:32,884 --> 00:05:34,589
Four: clone the repository,

126
00:05:34,589 --> 00:05:35,852
and copy the folder.

127
00:05:35,852 --> 00:05:36,926
You need a shell,

128
00:05:36,926 --> 00:05:37,431
and awk,

129
00:05:37,431 --> 00:05:39,326
which every Linux machine has.

130
00:05:39,876 --> 00:05:40,612
Five: again,

131
00:05:40,612 --> 00:05:41,961
no lab in this domain.

132
00:05:41,961 --> 00:05:43,309
The practice is plain,

133
00:05:43,309 --> 00:05:45,700
and the answers are on the next screen.

134
00:05:47,750 --> 00:05:50,225
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

135
00:05:50,775 --> 00:05:53,228
One: team one deploys three times a week.

136
00:05:53,778 --> 00:05:56,594
Two: team one lead time is about fifteen hours.

137
00:05:57,144 --> 00:06:00,088
Three: team one failure rate is 17 percent.

138
00:06:00,638 --> 00:06:03,113
Four: team two fails half of its releases.

139
00:06:03,663 --> 00:06:07,631
Five: after your change, a number moved, and you can say which one.

140
00:06:08,181 --> 00:06:11,082
Six: your file has four lines, in your own words.

141
00:06:11,632 --> 00:06:13,552
All six, and you can measure a team.

142
00:06:15,602 --> 00:06:17,373
Your turn, on your own machine.

143
00:06:17,923 --> 00:06:19,757
One: copy the demo folder.

144
00:06:20,307 --> 00:06:22,313
Two: run the script on both logs.

145
00:06:22,863 --> 00:06:26,297
Three: edit one row so a release fails, and run it again.

146
00:06:26,847 --> 00:06:29,791
Four: write your four lines, and tick the six points.

147
00:06:30,341 --> 00:06:35,013
Five: then think about your own team. Which of the four numbers would you improve first?

148
00:06:35,563 --> 00:06:37,398
Pause the video, and begin now.

149
00:06:52,948 --> 00:06:53,823
Quick check.

150
00:06:54,373 --> 00:06:56,101
Which two metrics measure speed?

151
00:06:56,651 --> 00:06:58,208
Which two measure stability?

152
00:06:58,758 --> 00:07:00,059
Why read them together?

153
00:07:00,609 --> 00:07:02,188
What does lead time include?

154
00:07:02,738 --> 00:07:08,498
Objective covered: MC16, objective six: the four key DevOps metrics.

155
00:07:09,048 --> 00:07:11,117
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

156
00:07:21,667 --> 00:07:23,822
Next time: finding the bottleneck.

157
00:07:24,372 --> 00:07:27,700
